1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:01,830
Your toast lands butter-side down.

2
00:00:02,430 --> 00:00:03,620
And it's not bad luck.

3
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:05,800
A physicist worked out the real culprit:

4
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:07,690
the height of your kitchen table.

5
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:08,880
And it's not just toast.

6
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:11,440
Your shoelaces, your earbuds, even your

7
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:13,180
spaghetti follow hidden rules.

8
00:00:13,770 --> 00:00:16,090
Scientists tested every one of them, with

9
00:00:16,090 --> 00:00:18,630
treadmills, spinning boxes, and two weeks

10
00:00:18,630 --> 00:00:20,090
of computer time...

11
00:00:20,090 --> 00:00:20,890
on a shower curtain.

12
00:00:21,190 --> 00:00:23,510
So why does everyday stuff seem to work

13
00:00:23,510 --> 00:00:25,830
against you? Let's start with that toast.

14
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:29,560
Push a slice of toast off a table,

15
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:30,820
and watch closely.

16
00:00:31,130 --> 00:00:32,150
It doesn't fall flat.

17
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:34,530
As it tips over the edge, it starts

18
00:00:34,530 --> 00:00:35,360
to spin.

19
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:38,180
In 1995, the British physicist Robert

20
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:40,210
Matthews worked out how much.

21
00:00:40,510 --> 00:00:42,570
From a normal table, the fall is so

22
00:00:42,570 --> 00:00:44,550
short that the toast only has time for

23
00:00:44,550 --> 00:00:45,660
about half a turn.

24
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:47,440
And half a turn means the side that

25
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:50,120
started on top, the buttered side, ends up

26
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:50,820
on the bottom.

27
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:51,780
So can you beat it?

28
00:00:51,780 --> 00:00:53,700
With a taller table, yes.

29
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:55,820
About three meters tall, high enough for a

30
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:56,590
full turn.

31
00:00:56,900 --> 00:00:57,920
Or push it off fast.

32
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:00,640
Toast that shoots off quickly doesn't spin

33
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:01,400
enough to flip.

34
00:01:01,870 --> 00:01:04,030
Matthews won an Ig Nobel Prize for this:

35
00:01:04,350 --> 00:01:05,930
a prize for research that makes people

36
00:01:05,930 --> 00:01:07,410
laugh, and then think.

37
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:09,860
Now look down.

38
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:13,200
Why do shoelaces come undone, even when

39
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:14,020
you tie them tight?

40
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:17,260
In 2017, engineers at the University of

41
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:19,380
California, Berkeley, filmed a runner's

42
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:21,330
shoes in slow motion on a treadmill.

43
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:23,480
Every time a running foot hits the ground,

44
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:25,820
it strikes at about seven times the force

45
00:01:25,820 --> 00:01:26,550
of gravity.

46
00:01:26,870 --> 00:01:29,130
Each hit squeezes the knot and loosens it,

47
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:29,990
just a little.

48
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:32,460
Meanwhile, the swinging leg whips the free

49
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:34,060
ends of the laces back and forth, like

50
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:34,850
a tug of war.

51
00:01:35,150 --> 00:01:36,710
Neither one alone was enough.

52
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:38,490
Stomping didn't undo the knot.

53
00:01:38,690 --> 00:01:39,870
Swinging didn't either.

54
00:01:40,190 --> 00:01:41,490
But together...

55
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:43,900
Once the knot started to slip, it fell

56
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:46,010
apart in as few as two strides.

57
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:48,020
They also found that a knot that twists

58
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:49,640
when you pull it tight is the weak

59
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:51,900
kind. A knot that lies flat holds on

60
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:52,600
longer.

61
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:54,180
But even the strong knot fails in the

62
00:01:54,180 --> 00:01:55,740
end. It just takes longer.

63
00:01:57,310 --> 00:02:00,390
Next mystery. You put tidy earbuds in your

64
00:02:00,390 --> 00:02:02,470
pocket. You take them out, and they're a

65
00:02:02,470 --> 00:02:04,170
knotted mess. How?

66
00:02:04,690 --> 00:02:07,790
In 2007, two physicists in California put

67
00:02:07,790 --> 00:02:09,570
a piece of string in a box and

68
00:02:09,570 --> 00:02:11,000
spun it for ten seconds.

69
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:12,280
Then they did it again.

70
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:15,670
And again. More than three thousand times.

71
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:17,760
Strings shorter than forty-six centimeters

72
00:02:17,760 --> 00:02:19,270
never knotted at all.

73
00:02:19,570 --> 00:02:21,490
But longer strings knotted in about half

74
00:02:21,490 --> 00:02:23,890
of the tumbles, some in complicated knots,

75
00:02:24,010 --> 00:02:24,730
within seconds.

76
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,860
The reason: the loose end keeps wandering

77
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:29,660
over and under the rest of the string.

78
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:32,080
Every crossing is a chance to tie a

79
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:32,680
knot.

80
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:34,520
Longer and floppier means more chances.

81
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,020
A very flexible string knotted in

82
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:38,980
eighty-five percent of the tumbles.

83
00:02:39,290 --> 00:02:40,970
So a long, floppy cable in your pocket

84
00:02:40,970 --> 00:02:42,950
is almost asking to be tied.

85
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:47,840
Try this. Snap one stick of dry spaghetti

86
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:49,550
into just two pieces.

87
00:02:49,850 --> 00:02:51,130
It almost never works.

88
00:02:51,710 --> 00:02:54,090
It breaks into three pieces, or more.

89
00:02:54,390 --> 00:02:56,750
The famous physicist Richard Feynman once

90
00:02:56,750 --> 00:02:58,590
spent a good part of an evening breaking

91
00:02:58,590 --> 00:03:00,460
spaghetti, trying to explain it.

92
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:03,640
In 2005, two French scientists finally

93
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:04,350
cracked it.

94
00:03:04,650 --> 00:03:06,810
When the stick snaps near the middle, each

95
00:03:06,810 --> 00:03:09,210
half springs back, and that sends a wave

96
00:03:09,210 --> 00:03:10,960
of bending down the pieces.

97
00:03:11,260 --> 00:03:13,020
The wave bends them even more than

98
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:15,620
before... and they snap again.

99
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:18,700
Then in 2018, a team at MIT found

100
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:21,100
the trick: twist the spaghetti first,

101
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:24,070
almost a full turn, and then bend it.

102
00:03:24,370 --> 00:03:26,630
The twist soaks up the snap-back, and the

103
00:03:26,630 --> 00:03:28,140
stick breaks cleanly in two.

104
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:30,060
They built a special machine to do it.

105
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:31,780
So, good luck at dinner.

106
00:03:33,350 --> 00:03:34,810
Now, the bathroom.

107
00:03:35,330 --> 00:03:36,930
You turn on the shower, and the curtain

108
00:03:36,930 --> 00:03:38,870
creeps in and sticks to your legs.

109
00:03:39,490 --> 00:03:40,190
Why?

110
00:03:40,450 --> 00:03:42,710
For years, people blamed hot air rising,

111
00:03:42,910 --> 00:03:44,270
or the fast-moving water.

112
00:03:44,580 --> 00:03:46,580
But the curtain gets pulled in by a

113
00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:47,520
cold shower too.

114
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:50,340
In 2001, engineer David Schmidt split a

115
00:03:50,340 --> 00:03:52,800
virtual bathroom into fifty thousand tiny

116
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:54,900
cells, and let his home computer run for

117
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:55,680
two weeks.

118
00:03:55,980 --> 00:03:58,160
All that, for thirty seconds of shower

119
00:03:58,160 --> 00:03:58,860
time.

120
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:01,620
The answer: the spray drives a spinning

121
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:04,040
vortex of air, like a little sideways

122
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:04,760
whirlwind.

123
00:04:05,090 --> 00:04:06,510
The middle of that whirlwind has low

124
00:04:06,510 --> 00:04:08,690
pressure, and low pressure pulls the light

125
00:04:08,690 --> 00:04:09,580
curtain in.

126
00:04:09,890 --> 00:04:11,730
His fix? Weights in the hem of the

127
00:04:11,730 --> 00:04:14,590
curtain. And yes, this won an Ig Nobel

128
00:04:14,590 --> 00:04:15,470
Prize too.

129
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:18,920
Spill a drop of coffee and let it

130
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:21,380
dry. You get a dark ring, not a

131
00:04:21,380 --> 00:04:24,260
brown spot. Why only the edge?

132
00:04:24,740 --> 00:04:27,540
In 1997, physicists in Chicago published

133
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:29,340
the answer in the journal Nature.

134
00:04:29,690 --> 00:04:31,770
As the drop dries, its edge gets stuck

135
00:04:31,770 --> 00:04:32,690
to the table.

136
00:04:32,990 --> 00:04:35,150
The edge dries fastest, so water flows

137
00:04:35,150 --> 00:04:37,180
outward from the middle to replace it.

138
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:39,740
That flow carries the tiny coffee grains

139
00:04:39,740 --> 00:04:41,840
with it, and dumps almost all of them

140
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:42,550
at the rim.

141
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:45,480
Then in 2011, a team in Pennsylvania found

142
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:47,360
a way to stop it: change the shape

143
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:48,500
of the particles.

144
00:04:49,030 --> 00:04:51,090
Stretched, egg-shaped particles grab onto

145
00:04:51,090 --> 00:04:53,070
each other at the surface, and dry evenly.

146
00:04:53,690 --> 00:04:54,390
No ring.

147
00:04:54,610 --> 00:04:56,330
Printers and paint makers care about this

148
00:04:56,330 --> 00:04:58,510
a lot. A ring is the last thing

149
00:04:58,510 --> 00:04:59,310
you want in ink.

150
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:02,460
Last one, at breakfast.

151
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:05,140
Why do the last few cereal loops in

152
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:07,280
your bowl clump together, or cling to the

153
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:07,980
side?

154
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:10,760
In 2005, two applied mathematicians

155
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:12,430
explained it in a physics journal.

156
00:05:12,860 --> 00:05:13,980
Milk doesn't lie flat.

157
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:16,360
Around every floating loop, the surface

158
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:19,120
curves up a little, making a tiny slope.

159
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:21,320
A floating loop drifts up that slope,

160
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:23,560
toward the next loop, and the two pull

161
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:24,260
together.

162
00:05:24,310 --> 00:05:26,670
The bowl's edge curves up the same way,

163
00:05:26,830 --> 00:05:28,910
so loops near the side slide over and

164
00:05:28,910 --> 00:05:29,740
stick to it.

165
00:05:30,090 --> 00:05:32,270
Same trick, much smaller: it helps

166
00:05:32,270 --> 00:05:34,290
scientists study how tiny parts could

167
00:05:34,290 --> 00:05:35,110
build themselves.

168
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:38,620
So it's not a curse.

169
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:41,980
Toast loses to table height, laces to

170
00:05:41,980 --> 00:05:44,580
seven g's of impact, earbuds to a

171
00:05:44,580 --> 00:05:45,490
wandering end.

172
00:05:45,990 --> 00:05:48,230
Spaghetti to a snap-back wave, the curtain

173
00:05:48,230 --> 00:05:50,890
to a tiny whirlwind, coffee to a flow,

174
00:05:51,270 --> 00:05:53,490
and cereal to the curve of the milk.

175
00:05:53,820 --> 00:05:55,520
Every annoying thing has a reason.

176
00:05:56,160 --> 00:05:58,390
Somebody just had to stop and measure it.

177
00:05:58,760 --> 00:05:59,840
Which one will you test first?

178
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:01,260
Tell us in the comments.
